<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehrangarh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1459</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mehrangarh Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehrangarh is a fort above the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1459 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore dynasty</a> as the new capital of Marwar. Built on a steep rocky hill, it is one of the largest forts in India, with massive walls and palaces of carved sandstone added by later rulers, and now houses a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehrangarh.jpg" alt="Mehrangarh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sanhitasinha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehrangarh_Fort_sanhita.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mehrangarh is a fort on a hilltop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words mihir, the sun, and garh, fort: it was first called Mihirgarh, a reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> clan’s mythical descent from the sun god Surya. Rao Jodha, the fifteenth Rathore ruler, moved his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a>, whose thousand-year-old fort no longer seemed secure enough, and decided the foundation of the new fort on 12 May 1459. It stands on a rocky hill nine kilometres south of Mandore, known as Bhakurcheeria, the mountain of birds.</p><p>According to legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> had to displace a hermit, Cheeria Nathji, who cursed the citadel to suffer a scarcity of water, and the saint Karni Mata of Deshnoke laid the foundation stone. A further tradition says a man called Raja Ram Meghwal volunteered to be buried alive in the foundations, and his family was granted land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The hill rises about 122 metres above the plains, and the complex spans 1,200 acres. Though the fort was begun in 1459, most of what stands dates from the time of Maharaja Jaswant Singh (1638–78). Its walls are up to 36 metres high and 21 metres wide. Entry is through seven gates. Jai Pol was built by Maharaja Man Singh in 1806 to celebrate victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and Bikaner, and Fateh Pol commemorates a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. Dedh Kangra Pol still bears the scars of cannonballs. Inside are palaces including the Moti Mahal, Phool Mahal and Sheesha Mahal, and the Chamunda Mataji temple, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> installed his favourite goddess in 1460.</p><p>The museum holds palanquins, howdahs, miniatures, musical instruments, costumes and a collection of arms, among them the sword of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, and a gallery of turbans. The ramparts keep old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, including the Kilkila cannon, and give a view of much of the city. Other galleries show the paintings of Marwar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and a royal red tent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Rudyard Kipling described the fort as “a palace that might have been built by Titans and colored by the morning sun.” The rock on which it stands has been declared a National Geological Monument by the Geological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Beside it, the Rao Jodha Desert Rock Park, created in 2006 and opened in February 2011, restores desert vegetation over 72 hectares. A crowd stampede at the Chamunda Devi temple on 30 September 2008 killed 224 people and injured more than 400. The fort has served as a film location and hosts festivals, including the World Sacred Spirit Festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mehrangarh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rao Chunda took Mandore around 1395, and in 1459 Rao Jodha founded Jodhpur and the hilltop fort of <b>Mehrangarh</b> as a new capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li></ul></section>
